#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147248 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3626bdd2-ec13-5d74-a570-ec65ba682723" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"}] :question/id #uuid "f888277f-085b-5c6a-b50c-8ffd9095bc53" :question/math-academy-id "e-2327" :question/problem "In a garden, there are $40$ flowers, $30 \\%$ of which are roses. How many roses are there in the garden?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of flowers in the garden, which is $40$ flowers. The percentage is $30 \\%$, and the part is the number of roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = 30 \\div 100 = 0.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 30% \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 0.3 \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = \\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 30% \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10} \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{40}{10} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147249 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "163d387b-3fd7-5f95-8245-5fb0184b371b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Decimal Percentage"}] :question/id #uuid "03c003db-96d2-5588-a1fa-944b9305cf6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2329" :question/problem "Dora works $800$ hours per year. She rests for $12.5 \\%$ ​​of her total working hours. How much time does Dora rest per year at work?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of hours Dora works yearly, which is $800$ hours. The percentage is $12.5 \\%$, and the part is the number of hours she rests.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = 12.5 \\div 100 = 0.125\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 12.5% \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 0.125 \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = \\dfrac{12.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 12.5% \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= \\frac{12.5}{100} \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 12.5 \\cdot \\frac{800}{100} \\\\\n&= 12.5 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147250 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"}] :question/id #uuid "f409e34f-c8f7-5e21-b024-a0c7012c496d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2330" :question/problem "Marcus has a tank with a capacity of $1,500$ liters. He decides to buy a second tank whose capacity is $130 \\%$ of the capacity of the first tank. What is the capacity of the second tank?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first tank, which is $1,500$ liters. The percentage is $130 \\%$, and the part is the capacity of the second tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = 130 \\div 100 = 1.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 130% \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 1.3 \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 1, 950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = \\dfrac{130}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 130% \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= \\frac{130}{100} \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 130 \\cdot \\frac{1, 500}{100} \\\\\n&= 130 \\cdot 15 \\\\\n&= 1, 950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3626bdd2-ec13-5d74-a570-ec65ba682723" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfaf78ba-56ea-5dbc-bf0f-d1fb29590063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144855} {:answer/id #uuid "40134deb-7389-5d68-97b3-20daad4c3816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144856} {:answer/id #uuid "4177f036-3101-5274-94c5-0432db971310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144857} {:answer/id #uuid "49127b0f-1888-5371-bc26-5a32fc6aa407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144858} {:answer/id #uuid "6165c10f-dd7b-5cc9-a196-5ed39588b90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144859}] :answer-field